<Layout title="How to Choose a Local Web Designer | Holy Webs" description="A nearby designer may understand your market and make collaboration convenient." canonicalUrl="https://holywebs.com/resources/blog/the-benefits-of-hiring-a-local-web-designer/"><div class="migration-page"><header><nav aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / <a href="/resources/blog/">Blog</a></nav><h1>How to Choose a Local Web Designer</h1></header><article><p>Original article by Travis Whatley. Adapted for the current Holy Webs website.</p><section><h2>Look for relevant understanding</h2><p>A nearby designer may understand your market and make collaboration convenient. Ask for examples showing how they learned a client’s services and customers. Proximity alone does not prove design quality or business understanding.</p></section><section><h2>Agree how communication will work</h2><p>Ask who does the work, who approves changes and how feedback is handled. If meeting in person matters, confirm availability instead of assuming it is included. Set expectations for response times and project milestones.</p></section><section><h2>Review the scope</h2><p>Compare proposals on pages, content, design, implementation, testing and launch support. Ask what the business must supply and what counts as extra work. A low headline price is difficult to evaluate without these details.</p></section><section><h2>Clarify ownership and maintenance</h2><p>Ask who controls the domain and hosting, how files and content can be exported and what happens if you change providers. Document ongoing maintenance, security updates and support charges. Do not assume these are included in the design fee.</p></section><section><h2>Check the customer experience</h2><p>Review examples on a phone and with keyboard navigation. Look for clear service information and practical contact paths. Ask how delivery and booking will be tested with permission before launch.</p></section><section><h2>Choose a working relationship</h2><p>Supporting a local business can matter, but choose based on evidence, communication and a clear agreement. Keep a comparison sheet of deliverables, responsibilities, costs and unanswered questions before making the decision.</p></section><section><h2>Plan your next step</h2><p>Start with your <a href="/resources/blog/defining-your-websites-purpose-and-target-audience/">website purpose and audience</a>, or <a href="/contact/">contact Holy Webs</a> to discuss your priorities.</p></section></article></div>
